      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(memory): keep batch failure attempts finite",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\n`recordMemoryBatchFailure` used `Math.max(1, attempts)`, so malformed non-finite values such as `NaN` could poison the failure counter. Once `count` became `NaN`, the disable threshold was never reached.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nFailure attempts are now normalized to positive safe integers. Malformed, non-finite, fractional, or unsafe values fall back to one failed attempt.\n\n## User Impact\n\nMemory batching can still disable itself after repeated failures even when malformed attempt metadata reaches the state update path.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `OPENCLAW_VITEST_FS_MODULE_CACHE_PATH=/tmp/openclaw-vitest-cache-memory-batch-rebase node scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/memory-core/src/memory/manager-batch-state.test.ts`\n- `oxfmt --check` and `oxlint` on `extensions/memory-core/src/memory/manager-batch-state.ts` and `extensions/memory-core/src/memory/manager-batch-state.test.ts`\n- `git diff --check`\n- Claude autoreview: clean after rebase to `upstream/main`\n\n## Proof\n\nThe regression tests prove `NaN` no longer poisons the counter and `Infinity` no longer immediately disables batching through an invalid attempt count.\n",
        "branch": "llagy007/memory-batch-failure-attempts",
        "head_sha": "c7284115b7c2dc3adde659c4ce4c14aa25e99aa2",
